INT. CORONER'S OFFICE, AFTER HOURS - NIGHT

Green linoleum. A desk lamp. A wall of filing cabinets, drawers half open.

DETECTIVE MIRIAM ACHTERBERG-OLUWOLE (50s) sits behind the desk in her coat. She has not taken it off. She holds a single photograph by its edges.

She sets it down. Picks it up. Sets it down.

She presses play on a micro-cassette recorder. Hiss. Then her own voice, younger, steadier.

MIRIAM (V.O.)
(recorded)
Day one. The body's identified. Walter Pryor, forty-four. Case closed.

She stops the tape. Rewinds. Plays it again.

MIRIAM (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recorded)
Walter Pryor, forty-four. Case closed.

She turns the photograph over. A name, in pencil, on the back. She reads it twice.

MIRIAM
It's the wrong body.

She stares at the lamp. Her jaw works.

MIRIAM (CONT'D)
(to the recorder)
You signed the form. You, Miriam.

She hits the red button. Records.

MIRIAM (CONT'D)
(into the recorder)
Day nine hundred and four. Reopen it.

She flicks off the lamp. The room goes dark, and only the red record light glows on her face.

MIRIAM (CONT'D)
So who's in the ground?
